Lets start 
Phase 1: Create the EKS cluster

Use the newest EKS version AWS supports in your region. Check it with:
aws eks describe-cluster-versions --region $AWS_REGION --query 'clusterVersions[0].clusterVersion' --output text
replace with the newest version from describe-cluster-versions in the eks/cluster.yaml

eksctl create cluster -f eks/cluster.yaml
Create the cluster. This takes 15 to 20 minutes
-----
Confirm nodes are ready and the OIDC provider exists:
kubectl get nodes -o wide
aws eks describe-cluster --name $CLUSTER --region $AWS_REGION --query 'cluster.identity.oidc.issu
-----------
Create a gp3 StorageClass as the default for Prometheus and Store Gateway volumes:

If the cluster already has a default gp2 class, remove that default first so only one class is the default:

bash
kubectl patch storageclass gp2 -p '{"metadata": {"annotations":{"storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class":"false"}}}'
kubectl apply -f k8s/storageclass-gp3.yaml

---------
Phase 2: Create the S3 bucket

Create the bucket with versioning, default encryption, and public access blocked. Metrics data is not usually sensitive, but these defaults are the right baseline:

bash
aws s3api create-bucket --bucket $BUCKET --region $AWS_REGION \
  --create-bucket-configuration LocationConstraint=$AWS_REGION

aws s3api put-public-access-block --bucket $BUCKET --public-access-block-configuration \
  BlockPublicAcls=true,IgnorePublicAcls=true,BlockPublicPolicy=true,RestrictPublicBuckets=true

aws s3api put-bucket-encryption --bucket $BUCKET --server-side-encryption-configuration \
  '{"Rules":[{"ApplyServerSideEncryptionByDefault":{"SSEAlgorithm":"AES256"}}]}'

----------
Add a lifecycle rule that

aws s3api put-bucket-lifecycle-configuration --bucket $BUCKET --lifecycle-configuration file://iam/lifecycle.json
-----------
Phase 3: IAM policy and IRSA
aws iam create-policy --policy-name ThanosS3Access-${CLUSTER} \
  --policy-document file://iam/thanos-s3-policy.json
export POLICY_ARN=arn:aws:iam::${ACCOUNT_ID}:policy/ThanosS3Access-${CLUSTER}

--------------

Create two service accounts bound to IAM roles. Prometheus pods need access for the sidecar uploads. Thanos components need access for Store, Compactor, and Query:

bash
eksctl create iamserviceaccount --cluster $CLUSTER --region $AWS_REGION \
  --namespace $NS --name prometheus-sa \
  --attach-policy-arn $POLICY_ARN --approve

eksctl create iamserviceaccount --cluster $CLUSTER --region $AWS_REGION \
  --namespace $NS --name thanos-sa \
  --attach-policy-arn $POLICY_ARN --approve

