Phase 11: Validate the pipeline end to end
kubectl -n $NS get pods,svc,pvc
kubectl -n $NS get pods,svc,pvc

Browse to http://localhost:10902/stores. You should see both Prometheus sidecars (each with a prometheus_replica label), the Store Gateway, and the Compactor. A missing sidecar almost always means the headless Service selector does not match the pod labels, so return to Phase 6.

Run a query in the Thanos UI with up. It should return one series per scrape target, with no duplicate series from the two replicas. If you see each target twice, check that --query.replica-label matches replicaExternalLabelName exactly.

Check that the sidecars are uploading to S3. The first block uploads roughly 2 hours after Prometheus starts, so wait that long before checking:

aws s3 ls s3://$BUCKET/ | head

----------------
Phase 12: High-availability failover test

This is the test that shows whether the design works. Use a query that runs continuously, then kill one Prometheus replica:

bash
kubectl -n $NS delete pod kps-prometheus-0

While the pod restarts, keep querying up in Grafana or the Thanos UI. The data should continue without gaps, because the second replica still scrapes every target and Thanos Query deduplicates the results. Note the time and the result, because this is the evidence that your HA design works.

